      "math_academy_id": "q-15941",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Find an interval where the average rate of change of $f(x)$ equals $0$ given the following table of values of $f(x):$\n\n| $x$ | $\\,1\\,$ | $\\,2\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ | $\\,6\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\,-2\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,3\\,$ | $\\,5\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "We have $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ if and only if $Δf=0.$ So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & Δf=0 \\\\  & f(b)-f(a)=0 \\\\  & f(b)=f(a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means we have to find an interval  $[a,b]$ such that $f(a)=f(b).$\n\nSince $f(2)=f(6)=3,$ we conclude that $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=0$ on the interval $[2,6].$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
